   "subject": "gamma-Poisson distribution",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi All,<div><br></div><div>I&#39;d like to fit a gamma-Poisson distribution to account for over dispersion in count data using INLA.</div><div><br></div><div>In its simplest form the model in the JAGS dialect of the BUGS language is as below:<br><div><div><br></div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\"> model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br> bIntercept </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">^-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">length</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Count</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">eCount</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> bIntercept<br><br>    eDispersion</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">ii</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dgamma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">/</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sDispersion</span><span style=\"color:#660\">^</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">/</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sDispersion</span><span style=\"color:#660\">^</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>    </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Count</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dpois</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">eCount</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">ii</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> eDispersion</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">ii</span><span style=\"color:#660\">])</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span></div></code></div><div><font color=\"#000000\"><br></font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\">The key feature is that the alpha = beta in the gamma distribution so that it has an expectation of 1.</font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\"><br></font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\">Any help or guidance would be greatly appreciated</font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\"><br></font></div><div><font color=\"#000000\">Joe</font><br><div><br></div></div></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "Pzw9nhqYAgAJ",
   "from": "Håvard Rue",
   "author": "9a89594e7fcb7aad",
   "date": "2016-09-07T06:18:48Z",
   "subject": "Re: [r-inla] gamma-Poisson distribution",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "you cannot directly. you can use a model like\r<br>\r<br>\teDispersion = exp(... + Gaussian.random.effet)\r<br>\r<br>which is about the same.\r<br>\r<br>Best,\r<br>h\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>On Tue, 2016-09-06 at 10:25 -0700, Joseph Thorley wrote:\r<br>&gt; Hi All,\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; I&#39;d like to fit a gamma-Poisson distribution to account for over\r<br>&gt; dispersion in count data using INLA.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; In its simplest form the model in the JAGS dialect of the BUGS\r<br>&gt; language is as below:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;  model{\r<br>&gt;  bIntercept ~ dnorm(0, 5^-2)\r<br>&gt;   for(i in 1:length(Count)) {\r<br>&gt;     log(eCount[i)] &lt;- bIntercept\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;     eDispersion[ii] ~ dgamma(1 / sDispersion^2, 1 / sDispersion^2)\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;     Count[i] ~ dpois(eCount[ii] * eDispersion[ii])\r<br>&gt;   }\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; The key feature is that the alpha = beta in the gamma distribution so\r<br>&gt; that it has an expectation of 1.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Any help or guidance would be greatly appreciated\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Joe\r<br>&gt; \r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
